        <descriptionSet>
            <!--description source based on field notes provided by Manfred Kunter-->
            <display>View of falaj section and palm trees in the background in Ghul oasis, Oman. This part of the falaj is cut in the mountainside rock of Wādī Ghul and rendered in cement.
            Often, the contemporary sand-cement render replaces the original sarūj, a traditional hydraulic binder made of calcined clay and lime, which was used in the construction of
            irrigation channels due to its Pozzolanic and water-proofing properties.</display>
            <description source="Kunter, Manfred">View of falaj section and palm trees in the background in Ghul oasis, Oman. This part of the falaj is cut in the mountainside rock of Wādī Ghul and rendered in cement.
            Often, the contemporary sand-cement render replaces the original sarūj, a traditional hydraulic binder made of calcined clay and lime, which was used in the construction of
            irrigation channels due to its Pozzolanic and water-proofing properties.</description>
        </descriptionSet>
